<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Sarir</h1><p class="st-meta">550 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Khunzakh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Christian mountain kingdom of the Avars in Dagestan, famed among Arab geographers for its golden throne and its power over the North Caucasus highlands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sarir took shape in the 6th century in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> lands of mountainous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a>. Arab writers say its name came from a golden throne said to have been given by a Sasanian king. Little is known of its early history beyond these later accounts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled clans of mountain farmers and herders and were reported to control many thousands of villages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rusta">Ibn Rusta</a> and al-Masudi describe a Christian ruler and church while most subjects followed older beliefs. Sarir was tributary to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-alania">Kingdom of Alania</a> through royal marriages, and fought the Muslim rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread into the mountains and the Christian monarchy faded in the 11th and 12th centuries. Its lands and traditions passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/avar-khanate">Avar Khanate</a>, centred on the same town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khunzakh">Khunzakh</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sarir.jpg" alt="Sarir" width="320" height="205"><figcaption>Image: Deni Mataev · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_about_year_1124.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>550 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khunzakh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity and local paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Avar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Sarir, Serir</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=sarir" aria-label="Sarir on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5810 664 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sarir lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6130 920L6131 811M6130 920L6131 921"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 811h0M6131 921h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6130 920h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6130 920h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=850&amp;focus=sarir">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
